# Solution — GIAM 6.5 Problem 2

## Problem

Find a bijection from the set of odd squares,

$ S \;=\; \{1,\, 9,\, 25,\, 49,\, \ldots\}, $

to the non-negative integers,

$ \mathbb{Z}^{\text{noneg}} \;=\; \{0,\, 1,\, 2,\, 3,\, \ldots\}. $

Prove the function is both injective and surjective, and find its inverse.

## Setup

The non-negative odd integers are $ 1, 3, 5, 7, \ldots $ — i.e. $ \{2k+1 : k \in \mathbb{Z}^{\text{noneg}}\} $. Their squares form the set of odd squares,

$$$
S \;=\; \{\,(2k+1)^2 \;:\; k \in \mathbb{Z}^{\text{noneg}}\,\} \;=\; \{1,\, 9,\, 25,\, 49,\, 81,\, 121,\, \ldots\}.
$$$

This *parametrization* is itself the bijection: every element of $ S $ arises from exactly one $ k \in \mathbb{Z}^{\text{noneg}} $.

## The Bijection

Define

$$$
f : S \;\longrightarrow\; \mathbb{Z}^{\text{noneg}}, \qquad f(n) \;=\; \frac{\sqrt{n} - 1}{2}.
$$$

Equivalently, $ f\bigl((2k+1)^2\bigr) = k $: the formula reads each odd square as $ (2k+1)^2 $, takes its positive square root $ 2k+1 $, and recovers the index $ k $.

A few values:

| $ n $   | $ \sqrt{n} $ | $ f(n) $ |
| :---: | :---: | :---: |
| $ 1 $   | $ 1 $ | $ 0 $ |
| $ 9 $   | $ 3 $ | $ 1 $ |
| $ 25 $  | $ 5 $ | $ 2 $ |
| $ 49 $  | $ 7 $ | $ 3 $ |
| $ 81 $  | $ 9 $ | $ 4 $ |

Well-defined: every element of $ S $ has the form $ (2k+1)^2 $ for a unique $ k \in \mathbb{Z}^{\text{noneg}} $, and $ f\bigl((2k+1)^2\bigr) = k \in \mathbb{Z}^{\text{noneg}} $. So $ f $ does map $ S $ into $ \mathbb{Z}^{\text{noneg}} $.

## Injectivity

Suppose $ f(m) = f(n) $ for some $ m, n \in S $. Write $ m = (2j+1)^2 $ and $ n = (2k+1)^2 $ with $ j, k \in \mathbb{Z}^{\text{noneg}} $. Then

$ j \;=\; f(m) \;=\; f(n) \;=\; k, $

so $ 2j + 1 = 2k + 1 $, hence

$ m \;=\; (2j+1)^2 \;=\; (2k+1)^2 \;=\; n. $

Thus $ f $ is injective.

## Surjectivity

Take any $ k \in \mathbb{Z}^{\text{noneg}} $. The integer $ 2k+1 $ is a non-negative odd integer, so $ (2k+1)^2 \in S $. Computing,

$ f\bigl((2k+1)^2\bigr) \;=\; \frac{(2k+1) - 1}{2} \;=\; \frac{2k}{2} \;=\; k. $

So every $ k \in \mathbb{Z}^{\text{noneg}} $ lies in the image of $ f $; that is, $ f $ is surjective.

Together with injectivity, this makes $ f $ a **bijection**. $ \qquad \blacksquare $

## The Inverse

Since $ f $ is a bijection, it has a (unique) two-sided inverse $ f^{-1} : \mathbb{Z}^{\text{noneg}} \to S $. The natural candidate, undoing the steps of $ f $, is

$$$
f^{-1}(k) \;=\; (2k+1)^2.
$$$

A few values:

| $ k $ | $ f^{-1}(k) $ |
| :---: | :---: |
| $ 0 $ | $ 1 $   |
| $ 1 $ | $ 9 $   |
| $ 2 $ | $ 25 $  |
| $ 3 $ | $ 49 $  |
| $ 4 $ | $ 81 $  |

To confirm this really is $ f^{-1} $, check both compositions:

$$$
\bigl(f \circ f^{-1}\bigr)(k) \;=\; f\bigl((2k+1)^2\bigr) \;=\; \frac{(2k+1) - 1}{2} \;=\; k.
$$$

For the other direction, take any $ n = (2k+1)^2 \in S $:

$$$
\bigl(f^{-1} \circ f\bigr)(n) \;=\; f^{-1}(k) \;=\; (2k+1)^2 \;=\; n.
$$$

So $ f \circ f^{-1} = \mathrm{id}_{\mathbb{Z}^{\text{noneg}}} $ and $ f^{-1} \circ f = \mathrm{id}_S $, confirming $ f^{-1}(k) = (2k+1)^2 $ is the inverse.

## Remark — Sequences as Bijections

The bijection $ f $ amounts to saying: the $ k $-th non-negative integer corresponds to the $ k $-th odd square (zero-indexed). This is a special case of a much more general pattern.

Any *enumeration* of a set $ A $ — that is, a list $ (a_0,\, a_1,\, a_2,\, \ldots) $ of distinct elements that exhausts $ A $ — defines a bijection

$ \varphi : \mathbb{Z}^{\text{noneg}} \to A, \qquad \varphi(k) = a_k. $

For our problem, $ a_k = (2k+1)^2 $, so $ \varphi = f^{-1} $ and $ f $ is the inverse "find the index" map. The same recipe shows that *any* countably infinite set is in bijection with $ \mathbb{Z}^{\text{noneg}} $ — that is the meaning of "countably infinite."
